      "message": "Here are some improvements from Gemini. **Please review the suggested names to make sure they actually match the Focaltech protocol.** If they are not correct, please come up with a different name.\n\n----\n\n### Part 1: Parameter Renaming\n\n**Rationale:** The names `para2`, `para3`, `para4`, and `sub_para` are opaque artifacts from legacy code. They force developers to cross-reference documentation to understand what a command is doing. By analyzing the hardware protocol, we can see these bytes serve specific, consistent purposes:\n\n* `para2` is predominantly a **Memory Address**.  \n* `para3` is predominantly a **Length or Size**.  \n* `para4` is an **Auxiliary Address** (used when moving data between flash and SRAM).  \n* `sub_para` holds **Flags or Modes** (like hash modes or reset types).\n\nRenaming these makes the function signatures self-documenting and eliminates ambiguity at the call sites.\n\n**Code Changes:**\n\n**1\\. Update `util/focaltech/ft_scsi.cc` (The Struct)** Update the underlying packed struct to reflect the true semantic meaning of the bytes.\n\n```c\n// Replace the old FtCmdFormat struct with this:\nstruct FtCmdFormat {\n  uint8_t opcode;\n  uint32_t address;      // Replaces para2\n  uint32_t length;       // Replaces para3\n  uint32_t aux_address;  // Replaces para4\n  uint8_t subcmd;\n  uint8_t flags;         // Replaces sub_para\n  uint8_t reserved;\n} __attribute__((packed));\n\n```\n\n**2\\. Update `util/focaltech/ft_scsi.cc` (The Implementation)** Update the implementation of the builder function to map the new parameters to the struct.\n\n```c\nstd::array\u003cuint8_t, 16\u003e BuildFtCmd(uint8_t opcode, uint8_t subcmd,\n                                   uint32_t address, uint32_t length,\n                                   uint32_t aux_address, uint8_t flags) {\n  std::array\u003cuint8_t, 16\u003e cmd_buf{};\n  auto* cmd \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cFtCmdFormat*\u003e(cmd_buf.data());\n\n  cmd-\u003eopcode \u003d opcode;\n  cmd-\u003esubcmd \u003d subcmd;\n  cmd-\u003eflags \u003d flags;            // Formerly sub_para\n  cmd-\u003eaddress \u003d address;        // Formerly para2\n  cmd-\u003elength \u003d length;          // Formerly para3\n  cmd-\u003eaux_address \u003d aux_address;// Formerly para4\n\n  return cmd_buf;\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: DRYing the Codebase\n\n**Rationale:** Currently, `updater.cc` repeats a verbose two-step process for almost every hardware operation: building a command (often passing trailing `0`s for unused parameters) and then sending it.\n\nBy taking advantage of C++ default arguments (which comply with the Google C++ Style Guide for non-virtual functions) and creating a unified `SendAction` wrapper, we can collapse 3–4 lines of boilerplate into a single, highly readable function call.\n\n**Code Changes:**\n\n**1\\. Update `util/focaltech/ft_scsi.h` (Default Arguments)** Add the new parameter names and assign default values of `0` to the trailing arguments.\n\n```c\n// Allow omitting trailing parameters when they are just 0\nstd::array\u003cuint8_t, 16\u003e BuildFtCmd(uint8_t opcode, uint8_t subcmd,\n                                   uint32_t address \u003d 0, uint32_t length \u003d 0,\n                                   uint32_t aux_address \u003d 0, uint8_t flags \u003d 0);\n\n```\n\n\\*\\*2. Add `SendAction` to `util/focaltech/updater.cc**` Place this helper function inside the anonymous `namespace { ... }` at the top of the file. It automatically injects the `kFtCmdAction` opcode, which is used by almost every command in this file.\n\n```c\nnamespace {\n// ... existing constants ...\n\n// Helper to build a standard kFtCmdAction command and send it immediately\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e SendAction(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, uint8_t subcmd, uint32_t address,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data, TransferDirection dir, std::chrono::milliseconds timeout,\n    uint32_t length \u003d 0, uint32_t aux_address \u003d 0, uint8_t flags \u003d 0) {\n  \n  auto cmd \u003d BuildFtCmd(kFtCmdAction, subcmd, address, length, aux_address, flags);\n  return SendBulkCommand(device, cmd, data, dir, timeout);\n}\n\n} // namespace\n\n```\n\n\\*\\*3. Refactor Functions in `updater.cc` to use `SendAction**` Replace the verbose build-and-send pairs with the new wrapper. Here are the most prominent examples:\n\n*Refactoring `FtErasePage`:*\n\n```c\nstatic std::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e FtErasePage(const UsbDevice\u0026 device,\n                                                int length, int addr) {\n  uint32_t pages \u003d FtDivRoundUp(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(length), kFocalConfigSize);\n  return SendAction(device, kFtSubcmdErasePage, addr, {}, \n                    TransferDirection::kOut, kErasePageTimeout, pages);\n}\n\n```\n\n*Refactoring `FtMcuReset`:*\n\n```c\nstatic std::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e FtMcuReset(const UsbDevice\u0026 device,\n                                               uint32_t target_addr) {\n  if (target_addr !\u003d kFocalRomCfgAddr \u0026\u0026 target_addr !\u003d kFocalBootCfgAddr) {\n    LOGE(\"Reset address invalid: {:#x}\", target_addr);\n    return std::unexpected(FtError::kParameterError);\n  }\n  // Passing 0s for length and aux_address to reach the flags parameter\n  return SendAction(device, kFtSubcmdSoftReset, 0, {}, \n                    TransferDirection::kSendOnly, kMcuResetTimeout, 0, 0, kSoftPor);\n}\n\n```\n\n*Refactoring `FtVerifyData`:*\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e FtVerifyData(const UsbDevice\u0026 device,\n                                          std::string_view file_path,\n                                          uint32_t target_addr) {\n  // ... [file reading logic] ...\n\n  uint8_t hash_mode \u003d (target_addr \u003d\u003d kFocalRomCfgAddr) ? (0x04 \u003c\u003c 4) : 0x04;\n\n  std::array\u003cuint8_t, SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH\u003e device_hash{};\n  \n  auto io_res \u003d SendAction(device, kFtSubcmdVerify, target_addr, device_hash, \n                           TransferDirection::kIn, kVerifyHashTimeout, \n                           firmware_data.size(), 0, hash_mode);\n  \n  // ... [hash comparison logic] ...\n\n```",
