      "message": "Main is very long and complex. Here\u0027s Gemini-generated code to simplify. Please review for correctness before using:\n\n\n----\n\nThis refactor applies two core principles of clean software design:\n\n1. **True Encapsulation (The Push-Down):** A hardware function should enforce its own physical prerequisites. If changing flash protection requires the MCU to be in bootloader mode, that rule is a fundamental property of the hardware operation, not a quirk of the CLI. Centralizing the guard inside the function prevents catastrophic accidental hardware writes if another developer uses the function later.  \n2. **Single Responsibility Principle (The Extraction):** Your `main()` function is currently acting as a parser, a USB initializer, and a business-logic router. By extracting the routing into an `ExecuteCommand` function, `main()` becomes a highly readable, linear initialization pipeline, and the router becomes a simple \"dumb\" switchboard mapping user intent to C++ functions.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Update Signatures in `updater.cc`\n\nFirst, modify the signatures of the worker functions to accept the `FtDeviceMode` so they can validate their own prerequisites.\n\n*(Note: `CheckEcBuildVersion`, `SwitchToBoot`, and `TestEcSwitchToRom` already accept `FtDeviceMode` in your original patch\\!)*\n\n```c\n// 1. FtGetFlashProtect\nstd::expected\u003cFtFlashWpArea, FtError\u003e FtGetFlashProtect(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, FtDeviceMode current_mode);\n\n// 2. FtSetFlashProtect\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e FtSetFlashProtect(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, FtDeviceMode current_mode, FtFlashWpArea wp_area);\n\n// 3. FtBackToRomBoot\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e FtBackToRomBoot(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, FtDeviceMode current_mode);\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Inject the Guard Clauses (`updater.cc`)\n\nMove the `if` statements out of `main.cc` and put them at the very top of these worker functions as early-exit guard clauses.\n\n**For `FtGetFlashProtect`:**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cFtFlashWpArea, FtError\u003e FtGetFlashProtect(\n    const UsbDevice\u0026 device, FtDeviceMode current_mode) {\n  \n  if (current_mode !\u003d FtDeviceMode::kBoot) {\n    LOGE(\"Get flash protect should only be done in boot mode\");\n    return std::unexpected(FtError::kInvalidMode);\n  }\n\n  // ... [existing I/O logic] ...\n\n```\n\n**For `FtSetFlashProtect`:**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e FtSetFlashProtect(const UsbDevice\u0026 device,\n                                               FtDeviceMode current_mode,\n                                               FtFlashWpArea wp_area) {\n  if (current_mode !\u003d FtDeviceMode::kBoot) {\n    LOGE(\"Set flash protect should only be done in boot mode\");\n    return std::unexpected(FtError::kInvalidMode);\n  }\n\n  // ... [existing parameter validation and I/O logic] ...\n\n```\n\n**For `FtBackToRomBoot`:**\n\n```c\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e FtBackToRomBoot(const UsbDevice\u0026 device, \n                                             FtDeviceMode current_mode) {\n  if (current_mode !\u003d FtDeviceMode::kBoot) {\n    LOGE(\"Current mode cannot switch to ROM via back command\");\n    return std::unexpected(FtError::kInvalidMode);\n  }\n\n  // ... [existing erase and reset logic] ...\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Create the Dispatcher (`main.cc`)\n\nPlace this function above `main()`. Because the domain functions now protect themselves, the switch statement drops all of its validation clutter.\n\n```c\n// Helper function to route the parsed command to the correct operation\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e ExecuteCommand(const AppConfig\u0026 config, \n                                            const UsbDevice\u0026 usb_device, \n                                            FtDeviceInfo\u0026 info) {\n  switch (config.cmd) {\n    case Command::kGetEcVersion:\n      LOGI(\"Tool version is v1.0.2\");\n      return CheckEcBuildVersion(usb_device, info.current_mode);\n\n    case Command::kSetFlashWp:\n      LOGI(\"Setting flash write protect to area: {}\", config.action_param);\n      return FtSetFlashProtect(usb_device, info.current_mode, \n                               static_cast\u003cFtFlashWpArea\u003e(config.action_param));\n\n    case Command::kGetFlashWp:\n      LOGI(\"Getting flash write protect area...\");\n      if (auto res \u003d FtGetFlashProtect(usb_device, info.current_mode); res) {\n        LOGI(\"Flash write protect area \u003d {}\", static_cast\u003cint\u003e(*res));\n        return {};\n      }\n      return std::unexpected(res.error());\n\n    case Command::kEnterBoot:\n      LOGI(\"Entering bootloader mode...\");\n      return SwitchToBoot(usb_device, info.current_mode);\n\n    case Command::kEnterRom:\n      LOGI(\"Entering ROM mode...\");\n      // Route to the correct ROM switch function based on the current mode\n      if (info.current_mode \u003d\u003d FtDeviceMode::kTest) {\n        return TestEcSwitchToRom(usb_device, info.current_mode);\n      }\n      return FtBackToRomBoot(usb_device, info.current_mode); \n\n    case Command::kUpdate:\n      LOGI(\"Updating firmware...\");\n      if (info.ec_bin.empty()) {\n        LOGE(\"No firmware file specified\");\n        return std::unexpected(FtError::kParameterError);\n      }\n      // The retry loop stays here because it is a higher-level CLI execution policy\n      for (int retry \u003d 1; retry \u003c\u003d 3; ++retry) {\n        if (auto res \u003d UpdateFw(usb_device, info); res) {\n          LOGI(\"Firmware update successful\");\n          return {};\n        }\n        LOGW(\"Firmware update fail attempt {}\", retry);\n      }\n      LOGE(\"Firmware update failed\");\n      return std::unexpected(FtError::kUsbTransferFailed);\n\n    default:\n      LOGE(\"Command not implemented.\");\n      return std::unexpected(FtError::kParameterError);\n  }\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Streamline `main` (`main.cc`)\n\nWith the dispatcher handling the execution routing, your `main` function shrinks down to a clean sequence of initialization steps.\n\n```c\nint main(int argc, char** argv) {\n  std::vector\u003cstd::string_view\u003e args(argv + 1, argv + argc);\n\n  if (!args.empty() \u0026\u0026 (args[0] \u003d\u003d \"-h\" || args[0] \u003d\u003d \"--help\")) {\n    std::println(ft_help::kCmdHelpString, argv[0]);\n    return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n  }\n\n  // 1. Parse Arguments\n  auto config_res \u003d ParseArguments(args);\n  if (!config_res.has_value()) {\n    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n  }\n  const AppConfig\u0026 config \u003d config_res.value();\n\n  // 2. Initialize USB Subsystem\n  UsbContext usb_ctx;\n  if (!usb_ctx.IsValid()) {\n    LOGE(\"Failed to initialize libusb.\");\n    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n  }\n\n  UsbConnectionDetails details;\n  auto device_result \u003d UsbDevice::Create(usb_ctx, details);\n  if (!device_result) {\n    LOGE(\"Failed to open device: {}\", ToString(device_result.error()));\n    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n  }\n  UsbDevice usb_device \u003d std::move(*device_result);\n\n  // 3. Initialize Device State\n  FtDeviceInfo info \u003d {};\n  if (auto init_res \u003d DeviceInit(details, info); !init_res) {\n    LOGE(\"Cannot find Focal device\");\n    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n  }\n\n  // 4. Execute the requested command\n  if (auto exec_res \u003d ExecuteCommand(config, usb_device, info); !exec_res) {\n    LOGE(\"Command failed: {}\", ToString(exec_res.error()));\n    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n  }\n\n  return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n}\n\n```",
